  <h2 id="what-this-course-is">What this course is</h2>

  <p>Welcome to MC 427, Social Media Analytics, the analytical anchor of the
  <strong>AI Social Media Strategist Certificate</strong>, a joint offering of
  SIUE and SIUC. The certificate is designed for students who want to enter the
  workforce as strategists who can supervise, govern, and account for
  AI-augmented analytical work.</p>

  <p>This course teaches you to use AI tools as collaborative partners for
  analyzing social media data and designing strategy, and just as importantly,
  to evaluate AI outputs with the critical eye of a strategist who is
  accountable for what gets posted in their name. We treat AI logging and
  governance as professional skills, not afterthoughts.</p>

  <p><strong>From the SIUE catalog.</strong> This course focuses on the use of
  analytics, including AI-powered tools, to evaluate and improve social media
  performance. Students learn to collect, analyze, and interpret data from
  multiple platforms using machine learning for pattern recognition and
  predictive analytics. Emphasis is placed on connecting metrics to
  organizational objectives, using insights to inform strategic decisions, and
  critically assessing the impact and limitations of AI in social media
  analytics.</p>

  <p><strong>Where it sits in the certificate.</strong> MC 427 is the
  <strong>methodological core</strong>: the course where you learn the
  analytical methods AI tools automate, so you can supervise and account for
  them when they do. Adjacent courses cover the workflow design that automates
  analytics (MC 425), creative content production (MC 328), AI advertising
  (JRNL 307), AI and misinformation (JRNL 438), and generative arts (AM 400).</p>

  <details class="syl-policy">
    <summary>Course goals, and how they map to ACEJMC competencies</summary>
    <p>By the end of the course, students will be able to do the following. Each
    goal expressly meets one or more ACEJMC professional values and
    competencies.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Apply AI-augmented analytical methods</strong>, including basic
        numerical and statistical concepts, to evaluate social media
        performance, audience behavior, and campaign outcomes. <em>(ACEJMC #7
        critical thinking in research, #8 numerical and statistical concepts,
        #10 apply tools and technologies)</em></li>
      <li><strong>Design data-driven social media strategies</strong> using AI
        tools as collaborative partners, applying tools and technologies
        appropriate for strategic communication work. <em>(ACEJMC #7, #10)</em></li>
      <li><strong>Critically evaluate AI outputs and your own analytical
        work</strong> for accuracy, fairness, and ethical implications, with
        attention to the cultural and demographic dimensions of AI bias.
        <em>(ACEJMC #3 culturally proficient communication, #6 ethical
        principles, #9 critically evaluate own and others' work)</em></li>
      <li><strong>Communicate analytical findings effectively</strong> in
        written, visual, and presentation forms appropriate for the
        communications professions, audiences, and purposes the work serves.
        <em>(ACEJMC #4 present effectively using tools and technologies, #5
        write clearly for the communications professions)</em></li>
      <li><strong>Govern AI usage</strong> through documented logging, oversight,
        and accountability practices that demonstrate professional ethical
        principles in pursuit of truth, accuracy, fairness, and service.
        <em>(ACEJMC #6, #9)</em></li>
    </ol>
    <p>The course goals above expressly meet ACEJMC competencies #3, 4, 5, 6, 7,
    8, 9, and 10. Competency #2 (multicultural history of communications
    professionals and institutions) is lightly addressed in Module 1's landscape
    lecture. Competency #1 (freedom of speech and press) is primarily covered by
    other courses in the major sequence and is touched only tangentially in
    Module 3 (platform terms of service, content moderation).</p>
