  <h2 id="what-you-earn">What you earn</h2>

  <p><strong>Primary credential: the AI Social Media Strategist Certificate
  (Credly badge).</strong> The certificate program issues a Credly digital badge
  upon completion of the certificate's required courses. MC 427 is one of the
  courses that contributes to that badge. The Credly badge is the primary
  verifiable credential the program offers; it is portable, shareable, and
  verifiable by employers through the Credly platform.</p>

  <p><strong>Industry certification, recommended and optional.</strong> Students
  completing this course are well-prepared to earn industry-recognized social
  media certifications. The recommended primary pathway is the <strong>HubSpot
  Social Media Marketing Certification</strong>, offered free by HubSpot Academy
  and completed in roughly five hours. Submit proof of completion during or
  shortly after the course for a small bonus credit (+10 points). It is a
  professional-practice extension, not a course requirement.</p>

  <p>Alternative or follow-on certifications, for information only:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hootsuite Social Media Marketing Certification</strong> ($199
      standard; check Hootsuite Academy for current student pricing). Strong
      industry recognition, covers multi-platform management.</li>
    <li><strong>Meta Social Media Marketing Professional Certificate</strong>
      (Coursera subscription, roughly five months). A long-form pathway with
      AI-integrated curriculum and an ACE credit recommendation; suitable as a
      post-course continuation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="ai-use-policy">AI use policy</h2>

  <div class="syl-note">
    <p class="syl-note-title">This course overrides SIUE's default AI policy</p>
    <p>SIUE's default policy is that AI use is prohibited unless expressly
    allowed. That default is <strong>expressly overridden for this
    course</strong>. AI use here is not merely permitted, it is
    <strong>required</strong> as a learning outcome. That override is specific to
    MC 427; it does not carry to your other courses.</p>
  </div>

  <p><strong>What is required.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Use of one or more generative AI tools as collaborative partners across
      all major assignments.</li>
    <li><strong>Logging</strong> your AI use in the AI Use Log that runs through
      every module: which tool, what prompts, what the AI produced, what you
      kept, what you changed, what oversight decisions you made, and why.</li>
    <li><strong>Critical evaluation</strong> of AI outputs in your own voice.
      Submitting an AI output verbatim without critique earns a low grade even
      if the output is correct.</li>
    <li><strong>Governance discipline</strong>: every analytical step that
      incorporates AI gets a documented decision about whether to accept,
      modify, or reject the AI's contribution.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>What is not permitted.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Submitting AI-generated work as your own without disclosure of AI
      involvement. This is academic dishonesty.</li>
    <li>Using AI to fabricate citations, data, or analytical results. The course
      teaches that AI hallucinates and that strategists are accountable;
      submitting fabricated material indicates this lesson did not land.</li>
    <li>Using AI to circumvent learning objectives, for example having an AI
      write your final reflection from your other submissions.</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>The strategist standard.</strong> In industry, a senior strategist
  is accountable for AI-augmented work that goes out under their name. The same
  standard applies here. You are accountable for what you submit, including
  whatever role AI played in producing it.</p>

  <p>The AI Use Log assignments are formative: they exist to build the habit,
  not to penalize early-stage workflows. Quality of governance reasoning matters
  more than quantity of logged interactions.</p>

