  <p><strong>On the final weeks.</strong> Thanksgiving week (Nov 23 to 29) has no
  module and no assignments. Modules 6, 7, and 8 each run for one week after the
  break, and the term ends Friday, December 18. Module 8 is a single focused
  week centered on the Final Pitch Deck; the AI Workflow Appendix opens in
  Module 7, and the draft peer review sits in Module 7, so neither compresses
  into the final week. Office hours continue throughout, and attendance is
  strongly encouraged for students with questions about the pitch deck or
  presentation.</p>

  <p><em>Schedule subject to change with notice. See Blackboard for the current
  version.</em></p>

  <h2 id="materials-and-technology">Materials and technology</h2>

  <p><strong>Textbook.</strong> This course uses an Open Educational Resource
  (OER) adapted from the AURA Lab's <em>From Vibes to Variables</em> methodology
  textbook. Until the AI Social Media Strategist edition is published, weekly
  readings are linked through Blackboard. There is no textbook to purchase.</p>

  <p><strong>Software.</strong> The course uses a mix of AI tools, social
  listening tools, and analytical software. All required tools are free or have
  free tiers sufficient for course use.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Generative AI</strong>: ChatGPT (free tier), Claude (free tier),
      or equivalent. Pick one as your primary; use a second for cross-checking
      on key assignments.</li>
    <li><strong>GitHub Copilot for Students</strong> (required for Module 5):
      free for verified students through the
      <a href="https://education.github.com/pack">GitHub Education Pack</a>.
      Works as an extension inside VS Code. As of March 2026, GitHub routes
      student requests through Auto mode (the system selects from OpenAI,
      Anthropic, or Google models on each request); premium-model selection is
      no longer student-accessible. Auto mode is sufficient for the analytical
      work in Module 5, and the variability is itself useful for the governance
      focus of this course.</li>
    <li><strong>VS Code</strong> with the R extension: free; replaces RStudio for
      the analytical work in Module 5.</li>
    <li><strong>R</strong>: free; install separately from VS Code.</li>
    <li><strong>Canva</strong> with AI features (free tier).</li>
    <li><strong>Social listening (free tier)</strong>: Brand24 trial, Sprout
      free, or Hootsuite free.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>You are not expected to become an expert with any single tool. Fluency
  across the AI-augmented workflow, plus the discipline to govern your own AI
  use, is the goal.</p>

  <p><strong>What you need to have.</strong> A reliable computer and internet
  connection are required. Specific to this course:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A webcam and microphone for the Module 8 Elevator Pitch video and remote
      office-hour appointments.</li>
    <li>A current version of a supported browser (Chrome, Edge, Firefox, or
      Safari), kept updated, so the AI tool web interfaces work reliably.</li>
    <li>If you use a pop-up blocker or a strict privacy extension, allow pop-up
      windows from the AI tools used in this course. Several tools open
      authentication or output windows that a blocker can silently suppress.</li>
    <li>A desktop or laptop computer for Module 5. The analytical work there (R,
      VS Code, and GitHub Copilot) cannot be completed on a phone or tablet.</li>
    <li>Storage: the course produces roughly 1 to 2 GB of analytical artifacts.
      Cloud storage (OneDrive, Google Drive, Dropbox) is recommended.</li>
    <li>Microsoft Teams installed (free for SIUE and SIUC students through the
      campus license).</li>
    <li>VS Code, R, and Copilot installed and authenticated by the end of Module
      1. Module 1 includes a setup walkthrough screencast and a confirmation
      submission.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Technical problems are not an excuse for missed deadlines, but they can
  often be resolved quickly. See <a href="#getting-help">Getting help</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="participation">Participation and interaction</h2>

  <p>Regular and Substantive Interaction (RSI) is required for distance
  education under U.S. Department of Education regulations. In this course, RSI
  takes the form of:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A weekly announcement video from me at the start of each module.</li>
    <li>Weekly office hours as a live, confidential support channel: in person
      Tuesday through Thursday afternoons, plus remote appointments through
      Microsoft Bookings.</li>
    <li>Substantive instructor responses on each discussion forum, on a weekly
      cadence rather than continuously.</li>
    <li>Personalized written feedback on graded submissions, on the timeline
      above.</li>
    <li>Optional 1:1 remote check-ins by appointment, particularly for Module 5
      (Statistical Analysis) and the Final Pitch Deck.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Your part is to engage with the course on a weekly cadence: read the
  assigned materials, watch the lecture videos, post to discussions, come to an
  office hour when you have questions, and submit work on time. Self-study is
  rarely sufficient for material this new; consistent engagement is the
  strongest predictor of a strong final pitch.</p>

  <p>This course includes an anonymous mid-course pulse survey and an anonymous
  end-of-course design-feedback survey. Your responses are read carefully and
  shape how this course runs, both during the term and in future offerings, so
  please take a few minutes to complete them.</p>

  <p><strong>Course recordings.</strong> All lecture content is pre-recorded and
  posted to the module before the module opens. Office hours are not recorded:
  under FERPA, recording a support session where students raise grades,
  accommodations, or personal circumstances would defeat its purpose, so those
  conversations stay live and private. Elevator Pitch videos in Module 8 are
  recorded by students and posted to the course discussion for peer review.</p>

